6.2.m:
When King Jalpa had attained absorption into that liṅga, O fair-faced one,
The god is known on the surface of the earth as Jalpeśvara, proclaimed by the gods;
he is remembered as granting enjoyment and liberation, and ever fulfilling desired wishes.
This power that destroys sins has been told to you, O goddess;
now hear of the god Jalpeśvara known by the name Kedāra.
Thus ends the sixty-sixth chapter, named 'Description of the māhātmya of Jalpeśvara', in the Māhātmya of the Eighty-Four Liṅgas in the fifth section, the Āvantyakhaṇḍa, in the glorious Skanda Mahāpurāṇa consisting of eighty-one thousand verses.
